Abstract
Exploring porous nano-structured materials has great significance for energy storage equipment. The metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) can be used as the outstanding sacrificial templates for electrode material of high performance supercapacitors due to their superior features that high specific surface area and tunable pore size distribution. However, the poor conductivity of MOFs is one of the biggest barriers to achieve high rate capacity and stable cycling performance. Herein, MOFs derived NiCo-layered double-hydroxide (NiCo-LDH) and nitrogen-doped carbon nanosheets (NC) on the flexible carbon nanotubes (CNTs) film are rationally designed, both of which as the binder-free electrodes can greatly improve the specific surface area and reaction sites. An asymmetric supercapacitor based on porous NiCo-LDH nanosheets on CNTs (CNT@NiCo-LDH) as the positive electrode and the NC nanosheets on carbon nanotubes film (CNT $@$ NC) as the negative electrode exhibits the maximum energy density of 37.4 W h/kg at the power density of $750~\mathrm{W/kg}$ , as well as a long-term cycling stability $(94.5\%$ capacity retention after 5000 cycles). Rationally design such combination is a meaningful process for energy storage equipment with excellent
electrochemical performance.
Keywords: NiCo-layered double-hydroxide, Nanosheets, Metal-organic frameworks, Carbon nanotubes, Hybrid supercapacitor
## 1. Introduction
With the high-speed development of society, the environmental pollution and energy crisis becoming increasingly serious. Designing and researching new friendly energy storage devices is one of key to solve the current crisis [1-3]. Supercapacitors, as a fresh class of electrochemical energy storage devices with higher power density, charming cycle life, and rapid charge and discharge, have enormous potential in many fields such as hybrid vehicles, industrial power, defense military, and storage systems [4-7]. However, the drawback of supercapacitors is relatively low energy density, which can hinder its large-scale development. Therefore, it is necessary to enhance the specific capacity and potential window to increase the energy density of the supercapacitors. Furthermore, electrode material is the core of the supercapacitor, to be especially, the type and morphology of electrode material is fundamental to enhance supercapacitors with high specific capacity [8-12].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of porous materials that have been widely used in many fields including gas separation and adsorption, catalysis, sensing, and drug delivery, which attributed to their unique characteristics, especially the higher specific surface area [13-15]. However, the poor conductivity of MOFs is one of the great challenges to achieve higher specific capacitance and higher energy density. Therefore, MOFs are always modified to improve its conductivity and specific capacitance to achieve a desired energy density [16-19].
Recently, two-dimensional (2D) nanomaterials are one of the most attractive candidates for energy storage applications, owing to their superior physical/chemical and electrical properties [20-22]. Among which, MOFs derived layered double-hydroxide (LDH) is a promising 2D nanomaterial to be used as the electrodes of the asymmetric supercapacitor for their variable oxidation states and higher electrical conductivity [23-27]. Moreover, MOFs derived carbon material, which is also especially attractive to the asymmetric supercapacitor due to their natural advantage that higher specific surface area and larger pore volume [19]. For instance, Liu et al. reported a novel structure that used CNT to thread the ZIF-8 derived N-doped porous carbon polyhedrons. When used as a binder-free electrode for symmetric supercapacitor, which showed a high energy density of 21.1 Wh/kg at 5000 W/kg [28]. A unique nanostructure of Co/Ni-MOF/CFP constructed by Deng et al. showed a well specific capacitance of $1044\ \mathrm{F/g}$ and the maximum energy density of the hybrid Co/Ni-MOF//AC achieved 28.5 W h/kg [29].
In this study, MOFs derived porous microarray of the layered double-hydroxide and nitrogen-doped carbon nanosheets on high conductive carbon nanotubes film, both of which used as the binder-free electrodes for hybrid supercapacitors are reported for the first time. The synthesis of Co-MOF is a facile and effective solution reaction, followed by hydrothermal to obtain the NiCo-layered double-hydroxide (NiCo-LDH). The MOF derived NiCo-LDH hollow structure possess high porosity and rich surface area, which can provide a good transport environment for electrons and ions.
Furthermore, the surface of hollow NiCo-LDH nanosheet has many tiny nano-size pieces, which facilitates the access of ions to electrode surface, effectively shortens the ion diffusion path and reduce the resistance of ion transportation. In addition, the nitrogen-doped carbon nanosheets derived from the Co-MOF can greatly improve the electron/ion transports kinetics due to its high specific surface area and conductivity. Furthermore, carbon nanotubes (CNTs) as the substrate can improve the electrical conductivity of the electrode material, owing to their distinctive one-dimensional nanostructure and excellent long-range conductivity. An asymmetric supercapacitor was rationally designed with NiCo-layered double-hydroxide nanosheets on CNT (CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH) as the positive electrode and the nitrogen-doped carbon nanosheets on carbon nanotube (CNT@NC) as the negative electrode. As expected, the hybrid device can reach a remarkable energy density of 37.4 W h/kg at power density of 750 W/kg, as well as a wonderful cycle life $(94.5\%$ capacity retention after 5000 cycles).
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Chemicals
All of the chemicals were analytical grade and without further purification. $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , 2-methylimidazole, ferric chloride $\displaystyle(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3})$ were purchased from Aladdin. And carbon nanotubes were purchased from XFNANO Company Limited, china.
## 2.2 Synthesis
## 2.2.1 Synthesis of the CNT $@$ Co-MOF nanosheets
Firstly, $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of CNTs solution $(0.15~\mathrm{wt}\%)$ was filtered to obtain a CNTs thin film. Secondly, $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ (0.582 g) were dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ DI water and stirred for $20~\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature, named as solution A. 2-methylimidazole $(1.3\ \mathrm{g})$ was dissolved in another $40~\mathrm{mL}$ DI water and stirred for $20~\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature, named as solution B. Then, the A was rapidly mixed into the B, stirring for 1min and putting a CNTs thin film $\cdot1\times2\mathrm{cm}^{2}\cdot$ ) into above mixed solution, keeping still for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . The CNT $@$ Co-MOF nanosheets can be obtained and then cleaned with DI water and ethanol alternately. Finally, the products were dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
## 2.2.2 Synthesis of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH nanosheets
$\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{20~mg}$ were dissolved in 20 mL ethanol and stirred for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ at room temperature. Then, pouring the mixing solution into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and putting above obtained CNT $@$ Co-MOF thin film into this mixed solution, and then kept it at $120^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . After that, the sample cleaned several times with DI water and ethanol, and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. Finally, the CNT @NiCo-LDH nanosheets were obtained.
## 2.2.3 Synthesis of CNT $@$ NC
The obtained CNT $@$ Co-MOF thin film was annealed in ${\bf N}_{2}$ flow at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\textup{h}$ 1 $\mathrm{{1^{\circ}C/\mathrm{{min}}})}$ . Next, the product was dipped into $3\mathrm{~M~FeCl}_{3}$ aqueous solution and held still for $6\mathrm{n}$ , and then the sample cleaned with DI water and ethanol, and finally dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
## 2.3 Characterization
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) were investigated on a D/MAX-2500 diffractometer with Cu Kα radiation source $\uplambda=1.54056\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) were recorded using an ESCALAB_250Xi X-ray electron spectrometer with A Kα radiation sources. The morphology and size of products were characterized from using the field emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, Hitachi S-4800). The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high resolution TEM (HRTEM) were carried on a F20 S-TWIN electron microscope at $200\mathrm{kV}$ acceleration voltage.
## 2.4 Electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical performance of the as-prepared product was evaluated by an electrochemical workstation (CHI 760E Chenhua, Shanghai) in 6 M KOH aqueous solution. For the three-electrode system, using the as-prepared CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH electrode as the working electrode, and using $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ electrode and platinum electrode as the reference electrode and counter electrode, respectively. The specific capacitance was evaluated by under equation:
$$
C=\frac{I\times\Delta t}{m\times\Delta V}
$$
where $C$ is the specific capacitance and its unit is $\mathrm{F}/{\mathrm{g}},I$ is the discharge current and unit is A, $\varDelta t$ is the discharge time and unit is s, m is the mass of the electrode and its unit is $\mathbf{g}$ , and $\varDelta V$ is the potential window and its unit is V.
The asymmetric supercapacitor was built up with the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH electrode (positive) and the CNT $@$ NC electrode (negative), which was investigated by a two-electrode system in the electrolyte of 6 M KOH solution. In order to achieve the
best performance of asymmetric capacitors, the positive and negative electrodes must observe the charge balance $(q^{+}=q^{-})$ :
$$
q=C\times m\times\Delta V
$$
where $C$ is the specific capacitance, $\varDelta V$ is the potential window, and $m$ is the mass of the electrode. To get $\boldsymbol{q}^{+}=\boldsymbol{q}^{-}$ , the mass balance of anode and cathode electrodes can be evaluated by the following equation:
$$
\frac{m_{+}}{m_{-}}=\frac{C_{-}\times\Delta V_{-}}{C_{+}\times\Delta V_{+}}
$$
Basis on the total mass of the two electrodes, the energy density $(E)$ and power density $(P)$ were calculated as follows:
$$
E={\frac{1}{2}}\times C\times\Delta V^{2}
$$
$$
P={\frac{3600\times E}{\Delta t}}
$$
3. Results and discussion
Fig. 1 manifests the fabrication process of porous NiCo-LDH load on CNTs film.
Firstly, the Co-MOF was directly supported on the CNTs thin film. Using ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ as a metal ion and 2-methylimidazole as an organic ligand, through a simple solution reaction in aqueous solution at normal temperature. After that, the color of the CNT $@$ Co-MOF film turns to purple. Secondly, CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH was obtained by the hydrothermal of the CNT $@$ Co-MOF in $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ethanol solution at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2{\ h}$ , allowing $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ to directly exchange part of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ on Co-MOF. Consequently, the color of the product turns to green.
The XRD pattern of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH thin film sample is exhibited in Fig. S1. It showed the characteristic diffraction peak demonstrated at 2θ of $11.7^{\circ}$ was corresponded to the (003) planes of NiCo-LDH, which is similar to the results of formerly reported paper [30]. However, just weak signal of NiCo-LDH was detected, this may be due to the following two reasons, one is the signal of CNT is too strong and cover up the other peaks, another reason is low mass loading and poor crystallize
of NiCo-LDH.
The composition and valence state of the element of the product were further tested by the X-ray spectrometer (XPS), which can be shown in Fig. 2a-d. From the survey spectrum (Fig. 2a), we can see the major characteristic peaks of the Ni, Co, O, C were demonstrated, which indicating the sample without any miscellaneous peak. The Ni 2p characteristic peak was shown in the Fig. 2b, the peaks at 872.7 eV and 875.2 eV were identified as the $\mathrm{Ni}2\mathrm{p}1/2$ that were consistent with spin–orbit characteristics of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ in NiCo-LDH. Meanwhile, the peaks at 855.1 eV and 856.8 eV correspond to the Ni 2p3/2 that also indicating the coexistence of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ . And concomitant two shake-up satellite peaks were observed at $879.4{\mathrm{~eV}}$ and $861.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ [31]. The Co 2p spectrum is shown in Fig.2c, the peaks at $796.3\mathrm{~eV}$ and $794.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the Co 2p1/2 that agreement with spin–orbit characteristics of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ in NiCo-LDH. Also, the peaks at $781.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $780.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to Co 2p3/2, indicating the coexistence of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ . while the two accompanying shake-up satellite peaks at $803.2\mathrm{eV}$ and ${786.1\ \mathrm{eV}}$ , respectively [32]. For the O 1s spectra of Fig. 2d, the O 1s spectrum can be convoluted into three components at 530.3 (O1), 531.4 (O2), and $532.8\mathrm{eV}$ (O3). The peak at the binding energy values of $530.3\mathrm{eV}$ (O1) that was attributed to hydroxyl ions of NiCo-LDH. The strong peaks at 531.4 eV (O2) is associated with the surface or internal hydroxyls and the oxygen of chemical adsorption. The weak peak at $532.8~\mathrm{eV}$ (O3) corresponds to physical and chemical water molecules in the material surface or bulk phase [33]. In addition, the C 1s spectra can be seen in Fig.S2, the peak at 288.8 and $284.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the C 1s that can be
assigned to the CNTs substrate.
NiCo-LDH nanosheets arrays on the CNTs film, at low-and high magnification, respectively
The morphological evolution of the samples was further observed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The nanosheets structures of the Co-MOF are synthetized on CNT film by the ordinary chemical reaction method. From the SEM images in Fig. 3a-b, it clearly shows that the numerous nanosheets of Co-MOF are uniformly load on the CNTs film surface. According to the scanning map of the Fig. 3a, we can see that all of the Co-MOF nanosheets are very uniform and clear in shape and size. To further explore the surface topography of Co-MOF nanosheets, the scanning magnification of the scanning electron microscope will be enlarged. From the magnifying scanning map in Fig. 3b, it is specific that the Co-MOF nanosheets have the extremely smooth surface. Meanwhile, the NiCo-LDH nanosheets were obtained by hydrothermal ion exchange reaction that the Co-MOF nanosheets were placed in a solution of nickel nitrate in ethanol at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 hours. It can be seen from the figures (Fig. 3c, d) that all the nanosheets are etched by hydrogen ions to become rough. As shown in the Fig. 3c. We can know that not only the surface becomes thicker but also the size is somewhat reduced. Furthermore, according to the enlarged view (Fig. 3d), it obviously shows the difference from the Co-MOF nanosheets that the NiCo-LDH nanosheets have a rough and porous surface.
In order to further observe the internal features of the NiCo-LDH nanosheets, the material was analyzed by TEM. From the result of Fig. 4a, more microscopic structure of the NiCo-LDH nanosheets are observed that it has the obvious change compared to the Co-MOF. After the hydrothermal reaction with the nickel nitrate solution in ethanol, the sample surface changed from smooth to porous. This is because the active nickel ion replaced part of cobalt ion on the Co-MOF during this hydrothermal reaction. This phenomenon was further proved by the magnified scanned image (Fig.4b). It is definite that the entire nanosheets of the NiCo-LDH were etched into a hollow structure, and its surface is full of tiny nano-size pieces. This phenomenon proves that the etching process is successful, which is consistent with the results of the SEM. The HRTEM images of NiCo-LDH nanosheets are displayed in Fig. 4c. The HRTEM images indicating that the lattice fringe of the NiCo-LDH nanosheet is 8.58 Å, which corresponds to the spacing of the (003) plane of the NiCo-LDH [30]. Furthermore, the element composition of the NiCo-LDH is affirmed from the images of the EDS (Fig. 4d). The result of the EDS images can indicate that the molar ratio of nickel and cobalt ions is well-distributed, which is about 1: 1.26. It demonstrates that the proportion of the elements of the product is extremely balanced. Additionally, there are Au element in the sample because in order to improve conductivity, Au is sprayed before the SEM test.
