To compare the electrochemical performance of the CNT@Co-MOF and the CNT@NiCo-LDH, a three-electrode cell was used to measure them in 6 M KOH electrolyte. The CV curves of CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH and CNT@Co-MOF at a scan rate of $2\mathrm{mV/s}$ with the potential window from 0 V to 0.5 V are shown in Fig. 5a. Apparently, the CV areas of the CNT@NiCo-LDH electrode are more than the pure CNT@Co-MOF electrode, illustrating the additional area specific capability of CNT@NiCo-LDH nanosheets arrays after exchanging part of cobalt ion on Co-MOF with nickel ion directly. The additional CV area of CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH electrode is due to merged the nickel ion and cobalt ion into the same metal organic frame, not only improves the electrical conductivity of the electrode by the synergistic effect of the mixed metal ions, but also increases the surface area of the electrode from the hollow and ultrathin NiCo-LDH nanosheets. Furthermore, scan rate dependence of the specific capacitance of CNT $@$ Co-MOF and CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH at a scan rate of 2 mV/s is shown in Fig. 5b. The corresponding specific capacitance of CNT@NiCo-LDH reaches 1213.7 $\mathrm{{{F/g}}}$ , while the specific capacitance of CNT@Co-MOF is only $60.3\mathrm{F/g}$ , which further proves the above explanation.
Fig. 5c is the CV curves of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH electrode at the various scan rates from 2 to $100\mathrm{mV/s}$ . All of the curves have a pair of redox peaks, illustrating the electrochemical process is the typical Faradaic redox process. As the scan rate increases, not only the pair of redox peaks move towards the positive and negative directions respectively, demonstrating the rapid transport and pervasion process of the electronic and ionic, but also the pair of redox peaks still keep the shape at the various scan rates of 2 to $100~\mathrm{{mV/s}}$ , showing the excellent rate capability of the NiCo-LDH nanosheets.
The typical GCD curves of CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH were measured at different current densities are exhibited in Fig. 5d. Clearly, the typical GCD curves certificated it is a Faradaic process, which is also a good proof of the analysis of the CV curve. Compared to the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH, the CV scan curve of the CNT $@$ Co-MOF and its corresponding specific capacitance map are separately exhibited in Fig. S3a-b. In addition, estimating the corresponding specific capacitance of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH based on the relevant GCD scan curve and the result can be shown in Fig. 5e. From the picture of the specific capacitance of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH, it shows the specific capacitance are 1159.2, 1131.2, 1078.2, 963, 835.2, and $776\mathrm{F/g}$ at the corresponding current densities of 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, and $10{\mathrm{A/g}}$ , respectively. Obviously, compared to the initial specific capacitance, the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH electrode displays a high capacity retention of $66.9\%$ at 10 $\mathrm{\bfA}/\mathrm{\bfg}$ , which demonstrated the electrode of the CNT@NiCo-LDH has a remarkable rate capability.
The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) diagram of the CNT@NiCo-LDH and CNT@Co-MOF are shown in Fig. 5f. All curves are constituted of semicircles and oblique lines at the high-frequency and low-frequency area, respectively [34,35]. Clearly, the X-axis intercepts of the hybrid CNT@NiCo-LDH electrode curves are lower than the CNT $@$ Co-MOF electrode in the high-frequency area, which shows the lower internal resistance $(R s)$ of the CNT@NiCo-LDH electrode. In addition, the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH holds a smaller diameter at the semicircle area than the CNT@Co-MOF, indicating the lower interface charge-transfer resistance $(R c t)$ of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH electrode. The CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH has the better conductivity than that of the CNT $@$ Co-MOF, which is ascribed to the ion exchange and etching reaction that the $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ exchange the part ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and then the generated $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ etch the Co-MOF become the hollow NiCo-LDH. Moreover, the CNT@NiCo-LDH has a more vertical line than that of the CNT@Co-MOF at a low-frequency area, showing the unique and porous NiCo-LDH nanosheet is good for the electrolyte diffusion and ion transmission, which is due to the peculiar hollow nanostructure possess more richer specific surface area and offer many ion transportation channels.
