In order to study the electrochemical properties of the CNT@NiCo-LDH, a asymmetric supercapacitor (ASC) was builded up with the CNT@NiCo-LDH (positive) and the CNT $@$ NC (negative), as shown in Fig. 6a. The morphology of the CNT@NC electrode was shown in Fig. S4a-b, which has the distinctive nanostructure that is beneficial to enhance the electrochemical properties of the ASC. In addition, the CV curves of the CNT $@$ NC were measured at the potential voltage window from $-1.0$ to $0\mathrm{v}$ with various scan rates for 2 to $100\mathrm{mV/s}$ are shown in Fig. S5a. The GCD curves of the CNT@NC were tested at different current densities of 2 to $10{\mathrm{~A/g}}$ and the corresponding specific capacities are shown in Fig. S5b. The CV and GCD curves of the CNT@NC all indicate that the negative material has very well electrochemical properties. Furthermore, the specific capacitance of the CNT $@$ NC can be estimated based on the corresponding GCD scan curve, which is shown in Fig. S5c. The homologous specific capacitances of the CNT $@$ NC electrode were 278.8, 159.6, 112.5, 90.4, and $82\ \mathrm{F/g}$ at 2, 3, 5, 8 and $10{\mathrm{~A/g}}$ , respectively. The CV scan curves of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH (positive) and the CNT $@$ NC (negative) can be measured at the scan rate of $2~\mathrm{mV/s}$ , and the result is illustrated in the Fig. 6b. The voltage of the CNT@NC electrode ranges from $-1.0\mathrm{V}$ to $0\mathrm{V},$ while the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH electrode
ranges from 0 V to $0.5\mathrm{V}.$
So as to achieve the higher electrochemical properties for the CNT@NiCo-LDH//CNT@NC ASC, the optimized voltage window has to be searched. As shown in Fig. 6c. The CV curves of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH//CNT@NC ASC were analyzed at the scan rate of $50~\mathrm{mV/s}$ . It is clearly that the CV curves will appear pronounced polarization when the voltage more than 1.5 V, thus the appropriate operating voltage is $1.5{\mathrm{~V}}.$ Under the suitable operating voltage, the CV curves of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH//CNT $@$ NC ASC were tested for every kind of sweep rates of $2~\mathrm{mV/s}$ to $100\ \mathrm{mV/\Omegas}$ , and the result shown in Fig. 6d. As the sweep rate increases, the CV curves and the peak current keep good development, indicating that the interface dynamics and performance of the material are favorable. At the same appropriate operating voltage (1.5 V), The GCD capability of the CNT@NiCo-LDH//CNT@NC ASC was studied at the corresponding current density of 1 to $10\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{g}$ and the result shown in Fig. 6e. The calculated specific capacitances of the ASC were 119.8, 90.9, 78.4, 67.0, 58.1, and $53.3~\mathrm{F/g}$ at 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 and $10{\mathrm{~A/g}}$ , respectively. Moreover, the cycling performance plays an important role on the electrochemical performance of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH//CNT $@$ NC ASC. The cycling curves of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH//CNT@NC ASC were evaluated at the current density of $3~{\mathrm{A/g}}$ , as shown in the Fig. 6f. For the cycling curves, the capacitance begins to increase as it cycles to the 3000th lap, which indicating that the material has been activated. After 5,000 cycles, the capacitance can still be maintained at $94.5\%$ compared to the initial capacitance. It shows that the cycle stability of the parallel white and blue LEDs lighted by two serious connected the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH //CNT@NC
ASC.)
Furthermore, the most worth looking forward to is that the combination of the CNT@NiCo-LDH//CNT@NC ASC can reach up to a maximum energy density of 37.4 W h/kg at a power density of 750 W/kg, is shown in Fig. 7. The energy and power density of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH//CNT $@$ NC ASC were quite remarkable compared to other related reports, such as Co-Mn LDH/Ni foam//AC device (4.4 Wh/kg at 2500 W/kg) [36], an asymmetric Co/Ni-MOF//AC device (28.5 W h/kg at 1500 W/kg) [29], ZnCo2O4 thin sheets// AC device (36.31 Wh/kg at 850 W/kg) [37], CNT threading nitrogen-doped porous carbon film (CNCF)//CNCF symmetric supercapacitor(21.1 Wh/kg at 5000 W/kg)[28], NiP@CoAl-LDH NTAs//AC device (37.18 W h/Kg at 0.45 kW/Kg) [38].
To further prove the practical application of the CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH//CNT@NC ASC devices, two as-prepared CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH//CNT $@$ NC ASCs was built up in series connection, which ability to illuminate the two parallel white and blue LEDs and the result as demonstrated in the inset of Fig. 7a. The result indicates the CNT@NiCo-LDH electrode material was synthesized by this simple and economical method has excellent electrochemical performance. The assembled CNT@NiCo-LDH//CNT@NC ASC has great practical application values and can play an important role in energy storage application devices in future.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we have synthesized a novel NiCo-LDH nanosheets on the flexible CNT film by two simple steps. In electrochemical performance test, we found that the unique material has great application potential for binder-free supercapacitors. The electrode of CNT@NiCo-LDH exhibits outstanding electrochemical performance of specific capacitance $(1159.2\mathrm{F/g}$ at $1\mathrm{A/g}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ) and rate performance ( $66.9\%$ retention at 10 A/g). Additionally, the ASC device based on CNT $@$ NiCo-LDH//CNT $@$ NC can achieve maximum energy density of 37.4 Wh/kg at a power density of $750~\mathrm{W/kg}$ , as well as terrific cycle life $(94.5\%$ retention over 5000 cycles). These excellent properties are ascribed to distinctive NiCo-LDH nanostructure with higher specific surface area, which can furnish great reaction sites and short ion diffusion length. Moreover, it is worth mentioning that the CNT thin film shows outstanding mechanical flexibility and superior conductivity. This work can provide a concise and clear method for the research of flexible and binder-free electrode in various energy-related applications. Compared to other similarly previously reported, our materials still need further improved. Therefore, our future work will improve the electrochemical performance of this material by doping other metal elements to form a multi-metal nanosheet loaded on the CNT film.
